<article class="env-proposition" id="04LW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LW">Proposition <span data-tag="04LW">9.6.2.13</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category containing a pushout diagram </p>
<div class="equation" id="06VY">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="06VY" href="/tag/06VY">9.19</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:pushout-of-semiorthogonal} \xymatrix@C =50pt@R=50pt{ C \ar [d]^{w} \ar [r] &amp;  C' \ar [d]^{w'} \\ D \ar [r] &amp;  D'. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> If an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is weakly $w$-local, then it is also weakly $w'$-local. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have a commutative diagram of sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@C =30pt@R=50pt{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D', X ) \ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C', X) \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C, X ) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D, X ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C', X) \ar [d] \\ &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(D,X) \ar [r]^-{ \circ [w] } &amp;  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( C, X), } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the square on the right is a pullback. Our assumption that $X$ is weakly $w$-local guarantees that the bottom horizontal map is surjective, so that the upper horizontal map on the right is surjective. Since (<a data-tag="06VY" href="/tag/06VY">9.19</a>) is a pushout square, the horizontal map on the upper left is also surjective (Warning <a data-tag="03FH" href="/tag/03FH">7.6.2.3</a>). It follows that the composite map $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}( D', X) \xrightarrow { \circ [w'] } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}}(C',X)$ is surjective. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></article>
